  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "K.A.Thomas vs The Food Inspector on 26 September, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 26Th Day Of September 2016/4Th Aswina, 1938 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 385 of 2010 ( ) -------------------------------- Crl.A 359/2004 of Addl.Sessions Court , North Paravur Cc 1534/2001 of J.M.F.C.-I, Aluva Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused: --------------------------------------------------------------- K.A.Thomas, Karothukunnel House, Chunanganveli,, Aluva. By Advs.Sri.Santhosh Subramanian Sri.Sherry J. Thomas Sri.S.Sreekumar(North Paravur) Sri.E.A.Thankappan Respondent(S)/Complainant/State: ------------------------------------------------------- 1. The Food Inspector, Aluva Municipality, Aluva. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor,, High Court Of Kerala. Public Prosecutor, Shri.C.K.Prasad This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 26-09-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. K.A.Thomas vs The Food Inspector on 26 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/152613998/ 1 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.385 of 2010 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 26th day of September 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 9.8.2001 at 2.45 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased 250 grams of beef curry from the petitioner. After complying with the legal formalities, one sample was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext.P11 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for the food item and hence, the sample was adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P13 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would show that the sample contained water soluble synthetic colour sunset yellow in violation of Rule 29 of the P.F.A. Rules and was therefore, adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW3 were examined and Exts. P1 to P18 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 was examined on the side of the defence.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has argued that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act, Ext. P13 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6 It was held by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector [2016 (2) KLT 390] considered the above question on a reference by a learned Single Judge. The Division Bench in Rasheed (supra) held that if an analysis K.A.Thomas vs The Food Inspector on 26 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/152613998/ 2 is required to initiate prosecution, there should be notified laboratory and validated method of analysis as mandated under Section 23(1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act for a successful prosecution as held by the Apex Court in Pepsico Holdings (supra). 7.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has argued that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act, Ext. P13 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "6 It was held by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The Division Bench of this Court in Rasheed v. Food Inspector [2016 (2) KLT 390] considered the above question on a reference by a learned Single Judge. The Division Bench in Rasheed (supra) held that if an analysis K.A.Thomas vs The Food Inspector on 26 September, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/152613998/ 2 is required to initiate prosecution, there should be notified laboratory and validated method of analysis as mandated under Section 23(1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act for a successful prosecution as held by the Apex Court in Pepsico Holdings (supra). 7."
  },
